# The payout engine refuses to start without every variable below. None has a
# default: a missing signer endpoint that falls back to something else, or a
# ceiling nobody set, is worse than a process that will not boot.

# --- Signing -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The key that holds the funds lives on the octez-signer host and never here.
#
# TLS only. Verified against octez-signer 25.1: this JSON API is served by
# `launch http signer` and `launch https signer`, both TCP; `launch local
# signer` and `launch socket signer` speak a different, binary protocol. There
# is no mode that serves it over a unix socket, so `unix://` is refused.
#
# Start the signer with, explicitly:
#   launch https signer <cert> <key>
#   --magic-bytes 0x03          only generic operations
# and WITHOUT --allow-list-known-keys, --allow-to-prove-possession or
# --password-filename. Unlocking is interactive at daemon start: one human
# action per restart, never one per payout cycle.
TAPS_SIGNER_URL=https://signer.internal:6732
TAPS_SIGNER_PKH=

# Point Node at the signer's certificate when it is self-signed.
NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/etc/taps/signer-tls.crt

# The CLIENT credential, for a signer started with --require-authentication.
# LEAVE IT EMPTY: this client cannot satisfy that check yet — the signer
# answers `invalid authentication signature` to every payload it builds. See
# src/chain/client-auth.ts. Closing this is a prerequisite for mainnet.
TAPS_SIGNER_CLIENT_AUTH_KEY=

# --- Ceiling -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The most one cycle may move, in mutez. Above it the run stops and a human
# approves it, or it does not happen.
#
# It is per CYCLE, not per run: the queue pays one cycle at a time and checks
# each against this, so a backlog needs no extra headroom. What does need
# headroom is a cycle where a lot of accumulated debt clears at once, and the
# check is against amounts + fees + burns, not amounts alone.
#
# LEFT EMPTY ON PURPOSE, unlike the two below. Twice the largest cycle you
# have actually seen is the rule, and that number is a fact about YOUR baker
# that nobody else can supply. For scale, the reference baker (2919
# delegators, 500k XTZ delegated) pays 28 057 421 mutez in a cycle, so
# 60 000 000 would be its number — copying that here would be a ceiling
# chosen by someone who has never seen your pool.
#
# Too tight is not conservative: it stops payouts until someone raises it.
# Absent, the process refuses to boot, which is the correct failure.
TAPS_PAYOUT_CYCLE_CAP_MUTEZ=

# --- The cut (RN-24) ---------------------------------------------------------
# K: a payment enters the batch only when what is owed covers K times the
# estimated cost of that transfer. Below the cut the amount is not discarded —
# it is the baker's debt to that delegator and accumulates until it clears.
#
# The cost is read from `estimate.batch()` every run, never written down, so K
# is the only part the baker chooses. Measured on the reference baker (cycle
# 1336, 2919 delegators): with no cut, 4.50% of the pool becomes network fees;
# at K = 1, 1.82%. A larger K pushes that down and makes the small delegator
# wait more cycles for a larger payment. Measure the curve on your own pool
# before choosing.
#
# A decimal is exact here ("1.5" is 15/10, never a float). K below 1 is
# refused: it would send payments worth less than the transfer that carries
# them.
#
# Chosen 2026-09-06 (Rafael): K = 2. On the reference baker almost the whole
# gain is in the first step — no cut -> K = 1 saves 2.68 points of the pool;
# K = 2 buys another 0.39 and costs the median held-back delegator six extra
# cycles. Past 3 the curve is flat and the waiting is not. A cycle is 24h on
# mainnet today, so those cycles are days.
#
# This is the number for THIS installation, not a law. The curve depends
# entirely on how your delegated balance is distributed, so measure your own
# before carrying it to another baker:
#   node scripts/measure-cut-curve.mjs <baker> <cycle> --fee <mutez>
TAPS_PAYOUT_MIN_FACTOR=2

# --- The queue (RN-28) -------------------------------------------------------
# How many cycles may be owed before the queue stops and asks. Above it
# nothing is injected: the owed cycles are recorded, the accumulated total is
# reported, and a human decides.
#
# It exists for one scenario: coming back from a trip to a wallet emptied in
# one go, because the system decided on its own to pay a week of cycles.
#
# Chosen 2026-09-06 (Rafael): 3. A cycle is `blocks_per_cycle *
# minimal_block_delay` — 24h on mainnet today — so 3 owed cycles is a long
# weekend of downtime, which catches up on its own; a fourth means something
# has been wrong for over half a week and deserves a human.
TAPS_PAYOUT_MAX_OWED_CYCLES=3

# --- Network -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by @tezos-suite/chain. Endpoints are configuration, never code; there is
# no built-in network list, and no protocol constant is named anywhere.
TEZOS_NETWORK=
TEZOS_RPC_URL=
TZKT_API_URL=
